<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariarathes I of Cappadocia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/322-bce">322 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariarathes I was the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Cappadocia who kept control of the region after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. In 322 BCE the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> invaded Cappadocia to install Eumenes, defeated Ariarathes and had him executed. His adopted son Ariarathes II later regained the land, and his line became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathid-dynasty">Ariarathid dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariarathes-i-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="Ariarathes I of Cappadocia" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ariarathes_I_of_Cappadocia,_minted_in_Gaziura.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ariarathes was born in 405 or 404 BCE to Ariamnes and had a brother named Orophernes. His family claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> and from Anaphas, one of the seven Persian conspirators who killed the Pseudo-Smerdis. Under Artaxerxes II he and his family served as minor officials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of Cappadocia, then governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datames">Datames</a>. After the assassination of Datames, about 362 BCE, Cappadocia was divided into a northern and a southern satrapy, in response to the power Datames had amassed. By the 340s BCE Ariarathes had succeeded his father as satrap of Northern Cappadocia, a territory that later became part of the Kingdom of Pontus, and he sent provincial troops with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> on the campaign to pacify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> invaded Persian territory, Cappadocia and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> became rallying points for the Achaemenid resistance. Mithrobuzanes, governor of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>, was killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Granicus</a> in 334 BCE, and the Macedonian-appointed replacement failed to establish his authority. Ariarathes's own territory was largely unaffected by the invasion, and he commanded troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE. After the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> he kept resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> from his base at Gaziura as an independent monarch.</p><p>He minted coins at Sinope and Gaziura with legends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, the imperial language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>. A coin from Gaziura shows a seated figure like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> with the inscription Ba'al Gazir, Lord of Gaziura. Mary Boyce and Frantz Grenet note that the figure could portray the Zoroastrian Ahura Mazda or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Alexander's death in 323 BCE, Cappadocia was granted to Eumenes, who could not dislodge Ariarathes. He turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>, regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-arrhidaeus">Philip III Arrhidaeus</a>, who entered Cappadocia with the royal army in the summer of 322 BCE. Ariarathes mustered local troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> but was defeated and captured, and he and most of his family were crucified that year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a> later seized Cappadocia, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>. About 301 BCE Ariarathes II restored Ariarathid control over Southern Cappadocia with Armenian help, and the dynasty ruled the Kingdom of Cappadocia until 96 BCE, when Roman intervention replaced it with the Ariobarzanids.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariarathes I of Cappadocia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ariarathes</b>, the Persian satrap, defied Alexander's successors until Perdiccas killed him in 322 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Kingdom of Cappadocia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cappadocia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=-332" aria-label="Cappadocia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ariarathes I of Cappadocia"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5323 4254h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5323 4254h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=-332">What was here in 332 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
